		<title>Dwelly Raises $170M to Accelerate AI-Led Property Management</title>
		<link>https://ventureburn.com/dwelly-raises-170m-series-b-ai-property-management/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Clinton]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 15:58:39 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Venture Capital]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://ventureburn.com/?p=203790</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Dwelly Raises Fresh Capital To Scale AI Property Platform  Dwelly raised $170 million to support its AI-first property management approach. The funding was co-led by EQT Growth and General Catalyst,</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/dwelly-raises-170m-series-b-ai-property-management/" data-wpel-link="internal">Dwelly Raises $170M to Accelerate AI-Led Property Management</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<h2><b>Dwelly Raises Fresh Capital To Scale AI Property Platform </b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Dwelly raised $170 million to support its AI-first property management approach. The funding was co-led by EQT Growth and General Catalyst, including an investment from existing investors s16vc, and backed by KKR’s Philipp Freise as well as Begin Capital, DVC, and some of the top European AI founders. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The deal, comprising $95 million equity and $75 million of debt from Trinity Capital, pushes the </span><a href="https://www.impactmybiz.com/blog/what-is-proptech-real-estate-digital/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">proptech</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> group&#8217;s total funding to over $260 million in less than a year, offering crucial growth capital to fund further M&amp;A activity and invest in its technology platform.</span></p>
<h2><b>Dwelly Uses AI To Transform Letting Agencies</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">An alternative way to modernise the UK renting market, Dwelly was founded last year by Ilya Drozdov, Dan Lifshits and Dmitry Khanukov is instead buying out and consolidating small independent letting agencies under its own AI-powered umbrella. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The firm uses this AI to handle tenant inquiries, onboarding and communication, maintenance jobs and rent collection and make sense of regulations. Human staff are kept in for areas where human intuition and experience remain essential, rather than doing routine administration. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Chief Executive Officer Ilya Drozdov stated Dwelly has been designed from day one with the premise that eventually artificial intelligence would take up any task done at the operating level. He explained that automation allows property professionals to become trusted advisers while AI manages routine workflows behind the scenes.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Dwelly says the model has significantly improved productivity. Property managers on its platform can oversee more than 300 properties each, compared with roughly 100 units under traditional operating models.</span></p>
<h2><b>Funding Supports Acquisitions And Platform Expansion</b></h2>
<div id="attachment_203792" style="width: 1322px" class="wp-caption alignnone"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203792" class="size-full wp-image-203792" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Dwelly-2.png" alt="Illustration of an AI-powered property services platform connecting letting agencies, automating rentals, and transforming tenant experiences through digital workflows." width="1312" height="736" /><p id="caption-attachment-203792" class="wp-caption-text">AI-powered property platforms are reshaping the fragmented lettings market by combining acquisitions, automation, and digital workflows to deliver faster tenant experiences and more efficient agency operations. Source: Created by Ventureburn.</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The UK lettings sector remains highly fragmented, with around 20,000 agencies operating nationwide. According to Dwelly, the largest 100 firms control less than one-third of the market, creating substantial opportunities for consolidation through acquisitions.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The company entered the market by acquiring Hull-based Lime Property before expanding its portfolio through additional purchases. Instead of preserving legacy operating systems, every acquired agency is integrated into Dwelly&#8217;s AI-powered infrastructure from the outset.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">General Catalyst Partner Zeynep Yavuz-Willson said the company is transforming the rental experience by allowing tenants to receive responses within seconds, schedule property viewings quickly, and complete contracts digitally without visiting physical offices.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">EQT Growth Managing Director Nils Petter Nygaard added that the future of property services will depend on operational innovation rather than simple agency consolidation. He believes artificial intelligence will fundamentally reshape how letting agencies deliver services.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">More News: <a href="https://ventureburn.com/claros-raises-55m-series-b-pfas-destruction/" data-wpel-link="internal">Claros Raises $55M to Accelerate PFAS Destruction Technology</a></span></p>
<h2><b>Expansion Plans Target Europe And New Financial Services</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Dwelly plans to use the new funding to continue acquiring UK letting agencies while strengthening its AI platform. The company also intends to automate more complex compliance and maintenance processes as it scales its operations.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Beyond acquisitions, Dwelly is developing additional financial products for landlords and tenants. Future developments and new services are set to include rent guarantees, legal protection offerings, financing tools and contractor marketplaces aimed at making it easier to buy and manage property.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Plans for international expansion into selected European markets are also on the table once the UK presence is solidified. In order to service the growth plans and product developments of the company, its staff is set to increase from some 300 employees to 1,500 by the end of the year. </span></p>
